I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided tungstate-analysis framework first, then identify candidate phases and carry the best model through GSAS-II refinement. I’ll preserve intermediate evidence and verify that the exported CIFs, profile, JSON, report, and `final.gpx` are mutually consistent.

The chemical inventory is Mn–W–O, fired at only 300 °C, so I’m treating residual MnO₂/WO₃ and low-temperature manganese tungstate products as genuine competing hypotheses rather than assuming complete MnWO₄ formation. The scan is a synthetic-style raw-count measurement with unknown zero/displacement, profile, background, and specimen effects; phase identification therefore has to rest on the full-profile match, not a few peak positions.

Peak indexing already gives a strong preliminary answer: the three intense lines at 23.44/23.93/24.69° are the characteristic monoclinic WO₃ triplet, while 29.01, 37.68, 42.19, 56.96, and 72.54° form the rutile-type β‑MnO₂ sequence. Their shared positive angular displacement indicates a specimen/instrument offset rather than mismatched lattices. I’m now discriminating among the available crystallographic records and checking whether any weaker peaks require a reacted ternary phase.

The first full refinement is computationally heavier than the peak search, but it has begun cleanly. I’m using staged refinements so that background/scale, angular calibration, lattice metrics, and phase-specific broadening do not destabilize one another; this is also the path that will leave a replayable final GSAS-II project.

Candidate discrimination is now decisive: the triclinic WO₃ record fits far better than the monoclinic/orthorhombic alternatives after the same refinement protocol (current Rwp ≈ 5.9% versus ≈ 7.8–23% for the closest alternatives). β‑MnO₂ remains independently required. I’m checking residual structure next—especially for a weak Mn–W–O product—and then will refine the physically allowed nuisance parameters without letting them mask a missing phase.

The first independent replay exposed an important issue: the lowest nominal Rwp project relied on a negative Gaussian width term that GSAS-II clamps internally, so its saved curve was not a trustworthy forward model. I’m correcting that by fixing nonnegative instrumental broadening and re-optimizing the physically meaningful size/strain terms; only replay-generated Rwp values will be used for the final selection.

I’ve stabilized the fit against an independent GSAS-II replay and am now choosing the final structure within the known displacement limits, then I’ll export and cross-check every deliverable against that same replay calculation.

The two-phase interpretation is now stable: triclinic WO₃ plus rutile-type β‑MnO₂. I’m constraining the low-symmetry WO₃ coordinate shifts to the laboratory generator’s stated displacement envelope; unconstrained oxygen refinement improves the residual slightly but drifts beyond what this experiment can support reliably.

The unconstrained cell/zero solution showed the same correlation as the atom coordinates: it reproduces peak positions but exceeds the stated reference-variation range. I’m therefore profiling the admissible cell-strain range while refining only the physically bounded zero and specimen displacement; this keeps the submitted CIF defensible rather than encoding calibration error into the lattice.

A higher-symmetry monoclinic WO₃ reference has a lattice closer to the unconstrained metric, so I’m testing it as a serious alternative rather than selecting solely on cell similarity. The decisive check is whether it can reproduce the full intensity pattern under the same physical profile and displacement limits.

The alternative monoclinic model remains substantially worse after matched-profile and coordinate refinement (replay Rwp ≈7.84% versus ≈5.55% for P‑1), while the P‑1 framework is also much closer atom-for-atom to the best reference. I’ve selected P‑1 WO₃ + β‑MnO₂ and am packaging the replay-consistent project, CIFs, profile, and uncertainty report.

Completed the GSAS-II refinement.

- Triclinic WO₃, P -1: 70.82 wt%
- β-MnO₂, P 42/mnm: 29.18 wt%
- Rwp: 5.546%; Rp: 4.065%; goodness of fit: 2.061
- No additional crystalline phase was supported. Fractions exclude any amorphous content; practical fraction uncertainty is about ±2 wt%.

